Being a Cyberschlocker, I registered "BLACK" last night: 100,000 shares
are available, at a cheap, cheap price of p.1 or best offer sellers
accept. (Hate those 5-character names cited in the rules, just as
Klaus! hates being called just "Klaus." But now I notice some
longer-than-5-character names and plan to sell HEx shares.)

To be serious for a moment (uh oh, true sign of leg-pulling), I coined
the term "BlackNet" in late 1987 when I read Phil Salin's business
plan for AMIX, the American Information Exchange. In several marathon
discussion with Phil and Gayle (Pergamit, his wife), I described to them
Chaum's 1985 paper, more recent "Crypto" papers on electronic cash,
and so on. The implications for information markets were clear.

It was clear that information markets would ultimately flourish in a
crypto-mediated cyberspace, essentially similar to that of "True
Names."

I scared a few people (they know who they are :-} ) by talking about
the things that a crypto-anarchic "BlackNet" could be used for: buying
and selling Stealth bomber secrets (it was very secret in 1987-8),
corporate inside information, and even hits on people. I didn't have
to _like_ some of the transactions--and there are positive ones as
well--to appreciate the significance of what was coming.

(And the "evil" or "unethical" uses are the most important ones to
think about, as they represent the likeliest points of attack on the
idea by critics, by governments, etc. If the idea can withstand the
withering blasts, the more positive aspects will survive as well.)

What is so satisfying to me is how today's technology now actually
supports most aspects of this set of crypto-anarchy ideas, albeit
sometimes only in cartoon-like outlines.


