Date: Thu, 30 Sep 1993 19:36:02 +0100 (BST)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: HEALTH: Burning your Health Card

Tim May writes: 	
>	Watching the Fuhrer's "Health Care" speech last week would have given
>	me an ulcer, and since ulcer-producing activities are verboten under
	:	
>	I have a question.
	:

Hmm.

As a visiting alien, currently in the US (my first visit ever) I'm getting
a real eyeful of the controversy the proposals seem to be generating.

What puzzles me is that there doesn't seem to be any actual
criticism of the medical system itself ...

As far as I can tell, medical prices in the US are driven up by two
things: the overheads of an insurance system which needs to make a profit
on its business, and the ridiculous costs of treatment. A friend of mine
went into hospital in Palo Alto six months ago. He spent a week there
while they dosed him up on corticosteroids to "treat" his Crohn's disease.
The cost of that week was apparently $25,000 -- a ludicrous sum, given the
base cost of the medication as about $25. Even having a skilled consultant
on hand 24-hours a day would cost less than $7000 in the UK! Indeed, the
cost of his treatment was about equal to two heart transplants at 
Papworth. 

It seems (to my somewhat naive eye) that the health thing would be much
less of a big deal if the costs were realistic. Whence the huge inflation
in treatment costs? Well, again, historically it seems to be connected
with the arrogation of the "right" to treat illness to a professional
elite. This was justified to the laity as a necessity, to exclude quacks --
but it would be wise to recall George Bernard Shaw's observation that every
profession is a conspiracy against the laity. By restricting medical practice
to a skilled licensed elite, a price cartel was effectively established.
Moreover it is impossible for that licensed elite to operate effectively 
today withou a huge support staff. Yes, a lot of the clinical support roles
you find in a hospital are vital to the effective practice of modern
medicine -- but as their cost and sophistication increases, the frequency
with which they are called on decreases.

It's the same in pharmaceuticals. (If you want to know my opinions -- as a 
former pharmacist -- I'm sure Tim will sell you my dossier for a fee ;-) We
now have a ridiculous situation where the FDA is trying to ban dietary
supplements and -- get this, spices -- on the grounds that they are
dangerous untested drugs. You can in principle be arrested in the UK for
cooking up a skin ointment containing common garden herbs in your kitchen,
and giving it to a member of your household.

I'm inclined to say that Tim's ire is misdirected -- shooting at someone
who is attempting to apply a skin patch to a skin lesion on a broken leg is 
an inappropriate response; an appropriate one is to try to fix the break. 
A better solution to the US healthcare paradox would be to deregulate medicine 
completely, and let the fallout settle. The main brake on the provision of new 
medical treatments would then be the risk/benefit ratio (medical malpractice 
suits tending to deter cranks) rather than the need to submit a 
fifty-foot-deep stack of forms to the FDA or CSM to get a new medication
approved. At the same time, a lot of more minor ailments would wind
up being treated by non-orthodox practitioners. I've seen a fair bit of 
the alternative medical community in action, and while I wouldn't go to
a homeopath with a broken leg or to an osteopath with cancer, they're
far more effective at dealing with psychosomatic illnesses (including
stress related problems, which account for a hell of a lot of days off work 
these days) than conventional "big medicine" practitioners.

More to the point, why don't we abolish hospitals?

Hospitals are disease traps -- if you don't believe me, look up the
meaning of the word "nosocomial". They require administration infra-
structures that are better handled at a lower level, and they need to
be centralized. Their historic origins were as work-houses and asylums
for the feebleminded; today they've become institutional temples, above
criticism, but relying on their own inability to treat root causes of
illness (as opposed to acute presentations) to maintain a steady flow
of revenue-producing patients.

The whole practice of medicine in the Western world today is repugnant --
hierarchical, elitist, exclusivist, and overpriced. Clinton is trying to
fix it, whether or not you agree with his method. (I don't.) But when
something is this broken, there comes a time when fixing it is a negative
sum game. Maybe it's time to ditch the whole thing and start over ...


-- Charlie
