Date: Thu, 30 Sep 1993 14:50:15 -0400 (EDT)
From: Mark Sulkowski <SULKOM@ubvmsb.cc.buffalo.edu>
Subject: PHIL: Free Will

 From: John K Clark <johnkc@well.sf.ca.us>
>I maintain that randomness, whether it
>exists in the human brain or not, has much to do with the
>question of determinism but nothing to do with volition.

	I agree.  A random, unpredictable will is no more a free will
than a deterministic, predictable will.  I consider free will to be
SELF-will.  That is, self-directedness.  As long as the brain is capable
of considering options, making decisions (choices), and then acting on
those choices ... that is really enough to claim "free" will, even if
knowledge of the environment plays a large role in decision making.

	What people are probably railing against in claiming _free_ will
is the notion of being totally controlled by exterior (or interior, but
non-volitional) forces.  People feel that they have options and make
decisions.  They don't feel that they are controlled.  If the outcome of
such decisions is claimed to be predictable, then they feel that they
somehow didn't really have options at all -- only the ones they happened
