I didn't see Tim's original post, so I'm excerpting the above from
Brian's reply. If I missed important context, I apologize.

First, when in the course of 2 days, HAM traded anywhere from between
p.01 and p10, you can imagine that this is not _just_ the fault of the
market. It is also the fault of those trying to _expose_ the fault in 
the market. There are many ways to correct this. Your offer of $ for
Thornes is one way (but not the only way. I've discussed others before,
but don't recall any reaction one way or another). One simple correction
could be to no longer allow transactions under the high bid, or over the
low ask price. The software could be modified to _first_ make transactions
based on high bid or low ask (it currently gives higher priority to orders
in a FIFO methodology it seems), and then, only in cases of conflict, look 
at which order was placed first. I _believe_ this is how real markets work.

What does this mean? (for the market-impaired: no condescendence intended,
especially since I'm no expert myself ;-)):
It means that, if the high bid is p6, and someone places a sell order at
p.01, even if there was a p.01 bid placed before the p6 bid, the p6 bid
will still be given priority, and the transaction will be made at p6. This
is a "hard-coded" way of assuming that people are playing the market to
make money (Thornes), not to artificially manipulate reputation values. In
the "real world" (sorry, no trademark symbol), if an investor isn't watching
the market and adjusting their bids, then TOO BAD. The HEx system is
protecting buyers and sellers right now. I claim they don't _need_
protection. Instead, we're all able to just lazily place our orders without
paying attention. It's kind of a defeat of the market principles which I
thought were being tested here.

The second point I wanted to make is that I don't believe reputation values
are based solely on the value of posts to this list. So, whether or not
my posts are "wonderful and profound" is not the only issue at hand. Given
enough visibility, other factors may be just as important. My own investors
are aware that I occasionally send out a notice of my self-alleged extropian
activities or actions off-list. Does it _matter_ what goes into a 
reputation? No, since the market will decide in any case. But I don't think
it is necessarily just posts on the list.

I would like to also say that I do support a "real-world" valuation of the
Thorne. However, I don't think it can be accurately determined via a
reputation in the market. That should get better as more services are
actually rendered for Thornes.

