> Does anyone know anything about candle-construction? 
> 
> Does the shape of a candle affect how long/how well it burns?
> 
> Would there be some reason why we might want to know its surface area or
> its volume (i.e., is comparing the surface area-to-volume ratios for
> different candles a way to predict how long they will burn?)

Yes to all of these. You also need to know the composition of the wax, 
since different waxes burn at different rates, and the thickness of the
wick. Candles are typically made of some combination of beeswax (from bees), 
tallow (from animal fat), and petroleum-derived waxes. I'll leave
it to one of our resident organic chemists to expound on the chemical
formula of waxes in general. 

Beeswax tends to burn brighter than other waxs, but is much more expensive.
A perfect candle has a diameter just large enough for its composition and
wick size so that the wax is melted evenly across the entire diameter and
at the same rate as it is vaporized and combusted. If the diameter is too
small, the wax will melt too fast and drip down the sides. If the diameter 
is too large, the outside edges of the candle will melt too slowly. This
will cause the combustion area to slowly sink down into the candle. As this
happens, more wax (i.e., the surrounding walls) will be heated sufficiently
to melt, and the wick will drown. Since this is a negative feedback loop,
it may take a very long time for the wick to actually go out, but it will
produce much too little light to be of much use.

